  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Thursday) \nIssued at 1233 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nMostly calm and tranquil conditions return by Sunday afternoon, \nlasting through Monday night. Morning fog over portions of eastern \nNM may interrupt this each morning however. Attention then points to \nthe next Pacific storm system approaching the region late Tuesday. \nCurrent numerical model guidance has coalesced on this storm system \nlifting northeastward across the Four Corners region late Tuesday \nand Tuesday night. This has trended precipitation associated with \nthis system being more focused over the northwestern third of the \nstate or so, potentially reaching into the Rio Grande Valley Tuesday \nnight. Wednesday through Thursday as now trended drier and breezy to \nwindy. \n\nLate week into next weekend favors what appears to be the season's \ncoldest and largest storm system rolling into the western CONUS. \nHowever, largest by geographic area doesn't necessarily mean the \nstrongest in terms of impacts, at least for New Mexico. There \nappears to be considerable uncertainty regarding the dynamic \nevolution of this system with a complex set of vortmax spokes \nrotating around the periphery of the closed low while it to tries to \nevolve into a Rex Block pattern. Nevertheless, an active weather \npattern is favored by next weekend.",
